  "query_preview": "/MONDAY THROUGH SATURDAY/...\nIssued at 320 PM CDT Sat Sep 19 2026\n\nAs the upper level low tracks out of the region, will still see the \ndeeper upper trough remaining across the western United States. This \nwill help pull a cooler air mass south from Canada, keep \ntemperatures on the cooler side to start the week. Based on current \nguidance, highs will struggle to get out of the 60s through at least \nWednesday across most of the region. By Wednesday, may see some \nslightly warmer temperatures across the west, with highs pushing \ninto the lower 70s. As mentioned, this is on the cooler side of our \nclimatological highs across western Nebraska for late September. \nThis is typically the week when we see average highs dropping from \naround 78 to 75 degrees. This will also bring cooler overnight lows, \nas well. In fact, Monday night into Tuesday morning, may see \nwidespread lows in the mid to upper 40s across all of western and \nnorth central Nebraska. \n\nWednesday night, an upper level shortwave is expected to track north \nof the region, which may bring an additional chance of rain showers \nacross the region. As this upper shortwave tracks out of the region \nby Thursday morning, expecting to see upper level ridging building \nin to close out the week. As there is a little uncertainty in the \namplitude of the ridge, may still see some changes to the long range \ntemperatures. For now, expecting a return to more seasonal highs, \nwhich again, would be around the mid 70s. However, if a stronger \nridge can develop, may see highs push just a tad warmer into the \nupper 70s. Ensemble guidance remains in consensus that probabilities \nof exceeding 80 degrees remains less than a 10 percent chance to end \nthe week, so even if we do get above seasonal, expect it would be \nvery slightly above.",
